          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does acid reflux tend to happen after meals?",
          "answer": "Acid reflux, or gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), often occurs after meals due to several factors. One major reason is that large or fatty meals can slow down stomach emptying. When food remains in the stomach longer, there's an increased chance for stomach acid to flow back into the esophagus, especially when bending forward or lying down.\n\nCertain foods and drinks can exacerbate this condition. For example, alcohol can relax the lower esophageal sphincter, which normally helps to keep stomach contents from rising. Additionally, carbonated beverages can create gas that distends the stomach, further contributing to reflux symptoms (source: \"Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)\").\n\nAnother important factor is the body's position. Symptoms tend to be worse after eating and when bending forward, or lying down. Understanding these triggers can help in managing reflux symptoms, which is often recommended through personal dietary observations to identify what specifically worsens an individual's symptoms (source: \"Eating with reflux: meal size, timing, and finding individual triggers\").\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
